  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "Let \\(U = 2 \\cdot 2004^{2005},\\) \\(V = 2004^{2005},\\) \\(W = 2003 \\cdot 2004^{2004},\\) \\(X = 2 \\cdot 2004^{2004},\\) \\(Y = 2004^{2004}\\) and \\(Z = 2004^{2003}.\\) Which of the following is largest?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(U - V\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(V - W\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(W - X\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(X - Y\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(Y - Z\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12a-2004-p6.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Compute each difference by factoring:\n\n\\(U - V = 2004^{2005},\\) \\(V - W = 2004^{2004},\\) \\(W - X = 2001 \\cdot 2004^{2004},\\) \\(X - Y = 2004^{2004},\\) and \\(Y - Z = 2003 \\cdot 2004^{2003}.\\)\n\nSince \\(2004^{2005} = 2004 \\cdot 2004^{2004}\\) exceeds each of the others, none of which reaches \\(2004^{2005},\\) the difference \\(U - V\\) is the largest.\n\nThus, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "Factor each difference to write it as a multiple of a power of \\(2004\\)",
        "\\(U - V = 2004^{2005},\\) while every other choice is a multiple of the smaller \\(2004^{2004}\\)"
      ],
